#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The Ferrata testnet's HTTP gateway, behind Caddy (which terminates TLS, caps request size and sets X-Real-IP).

  POST /rpc (and POST / on the rpc. host)  LEZ JSON-RPC, forwarded to the sequencer on loopback, with a method
                                            allowlist, batch, range and list caps, CORS, and limits (below)
  POST /faucet {"account": "<LEZ public account id>", "public_key": "<64 hex, for an account never used>"}
  GET  /faucet   the faucet's terms, its account and balance (cached for a few seconds)
  GET  /relayer/gas-drop        the network's gas-drop relayer (RFP-022 U.5, Priv.7): its LEZ account, the guest's image
                                id, the native LEZ per drop, the inbox and the guest's URL; `ferrata claim` reads it by
                                default (from its sequencer's origin). 404 when GASDROP_INFO is unset; 503 when paused
  GET  /relayer/gas-drop/guest  the gas-drop guest (ELF) the relayer verifies receipts of (the tool checks its image id
                                against the ids it pins)
  POST /relayer/gas-drop        a gas-drop job ({"kind": "gas-drop", "receipt", "oneTimeNpk", "oneTimeVpk"}) into the
                                relayer's inbox (GASDROP_INBOX), after its checks (below); the relayer
                                (`ferrata-relayer --gas-drops-only`) verifies the receipt and sends one credit per deposit

Limits:
- per IP, a token bucket charged by work: one token per request, a getBlockRange one more per GATEWAY_RANGE_PER_TOKEN
  blocks of its span (at most GATEWAY_MAX_RANGE: 50, the wallet's page; the explorer is configured to match), a list
  parameter (getProofsAndRoot, getAccountsNonces; at most GATEWAY_MAX_LIST entries) one more per entry beyond
  GATEWAY_LIST_FREE; one token is taken before the body is read;
- response bytes: no answer above GATEWAY_MAX_RESPONSE; a byte budget per IP and one for everyone together (it bounds
  what the RPC can cost in egress). An IP's budget is at most GATEWAY_BYTES_SHARE of the network-wide one (rate and
  burst), so that it takes 1/share clients to exhaust that. A request goes on only while its IP's budget is positive
  (and holds what it needs, below), GATEWAY_BYTES_RESERVE (at most the answer cap) held from it while the request is
  forwarded. An answer is sent only if each budget can pay for it at that moment: it holds at least the answer's size,
  or it is full (an answer larger than a budget's burst goes alone, then the budget repays it); else 429 with the time
  to wait, nothing sent. So the bytes one IP is sent in any interval are at most its budget's burst plus its refill,
  or one answer when its budget was full, however many answers are in flight at once. An answer the sequencer built
  and the gateway did not send (over the cap, or not paid for) is charged to its IP all the same, for the bytes the
  sequencer produced, and that IP's next request goes on only once its budget holds that size again (or is full): the
  sequencer builds at most one answer an IP cannot pay for before the IP's budget has refilled by its size, and a
  client that waits Retry-After gets its answer. Answers of at most GATEWAY_SMALL_ANSWER bytes are not held back by
  the network-wide budget but by one of their own (GATEWAY_SMALL_GLOBAL_*, every caller together), and charged to
  both: a few heavy clients cannot stop everyone's small calls, and every answer together stays within the
  network-wide rate (plus the two bursts);
- sendTransaction per IP and for the whole network (the operator's own services, such as the updater, post to the
  sequencer directly and keep most of the 20-transaction blocks);
- sockets time out; at most GATEWAY_PER_IP_INFLIGHT requests per IP and GATEWAY_MAX_INFLIGHT in all are handled at
  once, the per-IP cap checked first, so that one client cannot hold every slot (Caddy reads each body whole before
  passing it on, so a slow sender holds none);
- the faucet: its per-IP bucket comes before any work; one transfer at a time, at most FAUCET_PER_IP_WAITING claims
  per IP and FAUCET_MAX_WAITING in all waiting (the rest get 429 at once, so that claims cannot take the RPC's slots
  and one client cannot take the faucet's); failed claims count against the IP and its /24 (IPv6 /64); a transfer that
  is not in a block yet may still settle, so it also counts against its account and the hourly total. A claim is
  written to the ledger (pending) before the tool runs, with the path of its transfer's record; the tool writes that
  record (the signed transaction's hash, bytes and nonce) before it sends the transaction. Whether a claim paid is read
  from its own transaction, never from the account's balance (another source may pay the same amount): in a block and
  applied, it paid; reverted, or its nonce spent by another transaction (it never can be applied), or no record (nothing
  was signed), it failed; else it is still pending: its very bytes are sent again once older than FAUCET_RESEND_AFTER
  (the same nonce: at most one is ever applied). An account with a claim still pending is refused until that claim is
  decided, which its next claim looks up first. No claim is made with a tool that does not record its transfers. The
  tool's output is logged, never returned; claims older than FAUCET_KEEP_DAYS are deleted with their records.

- gas drops: a job is at most GASDROP_MAX_BODY bytes, of the exact shape above, its receipt's journal 212 bytes of the
  gas-drop statement naming this relayer; one job per deposit nullifier N held at a time (409 while the inbox holds it);
  at most GASDROP_MAX_PENDING in the inbox (429); per IP GASDROP_PER_IP_PER_DAY jobs, per /24 (IPv6 /64)
  GASDROP_PER_NET_PER_DAY, every caller together GASDROP_GLOBAL_PER_HOUR (429; kept in memory: a restart forgets them).
  The bound that matters is the relayer's: one drop per N, and a receipt proves a deposit under a root the zone
  imported, so a drop costs its asker a deposit on Ethereum (a denomination and the vault's fees).

Standard library only. Configuration: environment (see DEFAULTS); the faucet's ledger is a SQLite file.
"""
import http.server
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import re
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import threading
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request

